Yes. For your Learner UI → APIM → multiple AKS microservices architecture, I’d implement correlation IDs at the first server-side entry point, then propagate them through every downstream HTTP call and message.
A clean flow is:
Browser
   │
   │ HTTP request
   ▼
Learner UI / BFF (.NET)
   │
   │ X-Correlation-ID: 9d36...
   ▼
APIM
   │
   ├────► Learner API
   │         │
   │         └────► Progress API
   │
   └────► Course API
             │
             └────► Content API

Every service logs:
CorrelationId = 9d36...

1. Generate it in the Learner UI's .NET backend
If your Learner UI is ASP.NET Core MVC/Blazor Server, middleware is a good place:
public class CorrelationIdMiddleware
{
    private const string HeaderName = "X-Correlation-ID";
    private readonly RequestDelegate _next;

    public CorrelationIdMiddleware(RequestDelegate next)
    {
        _next = next;
    }

    public async Task InvokeAsync(
        HttpContext context,
        ILogger<CorrelationIdMiddleware> logger)
    {
        var correlationId =
            context.Request.Headers.TryGetValue(HeaderName, out var existing)
                ? existing.ToString()
                : Guid.NewGuid().ToString();

        context.Items["CorrelationId"] = correlationId;

        context.Response.Headers[HeaderName] = correlationId;

        using (logger.BeginScope(new Dictionary<string, object>
        {
            ["CorrelationId"] = correlationId
        }))
        {
            await _next(context);
        }
    }
}

Register it:
app.UseMiddleware<CorrelationIdMiddleware>();

2. Automatically propagate it to downstream microservices
Don't manually add the header everywhere. Use a DelegatingHandler:
public class CorrelationIdHandler : DelegatingHandler
{
    private readonly IHttpContextAccessor _httpContextAccessor;

    public CorrelationIdHandler(IHttpContextAccessor httpContextAccessor)
    {
        _httpContextAccessor = httpContextAccessor;
    }

    protected override async Task<HttpResponseMessage> SendAsync(
        HttpRequestMessage request,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken)
    {
        var context = _httpContextAccessor.HttpContext;

        if (context?.Items["CorrelationId"] is string correlationId)
        {
            request.Headers.TryAddWithoutValidation(
                "X-Correlation-ID",
                correlationId);
        }

        return await base.SendAsync(request, cancellationToken);
    }
}

Then:
builder.Services.AddHttpContextAccessor();
builder.Services.AddTransient<CorrelationIdHandler>();

builder.Services
    .AddHttpClient("LearnerApi")
    .AddHttpMessageHandler<CorrelationIdHandler>();

Now:
var client = httpClientFactory.CreateClient("LearnerApi");

await client.GetAsync("/api/courses");

automatically becomes:
GET /api/courses

X-Correlation-ID: 9d36e45a-...

3. Every microservice should accept and propagate it
Use essentially the same middleware in each AKS service.
The important rule is:
If X-Correlation-ID exists
    → keep it

If it doesn't exist
    → create it

So if Learner API calls Progress API:
Learner UI
CorrelationId = ABC123
       ↓
APIM
CorrelationId = ABC123
       ↓
Learner API
CorrelationId = ABC123
       ↓
Progress API
CorrelationId = ABC123
       ↓
SQL / logs
CorrelationId = ABC123

Don't generate a new ID at every microservice.
4. Include it in structured logging
Because you're using Datadog, this becomes particularly useful.
Instead of:
Getting learner courses
Calling progress API
Course retrieval completed

you want structured properties:
{
  "service": "learner-api",
  "correlationId": "ABC123",
  "learnerId": "12345",
  "message": "Getting learner courses"
}

and downstream:
{
  "service": "progress-api",
  "correlationId": "ABC123",
  "message": "Retrieving learner progress"
}

Then searching Datadog for:
@correlationId:ABC123

shows the entire transaction across services.
5. APIM should preserve the header
I'd explicitly ensure it exists in your APIM inbound policy too:
<inbound>
    <base />

    <set-header name="X-Correlation-ID"
                exists-action="skip">
        <value>@(Guid.NewGuid().ToString())</value>
    </set-header>

</inbound>

exists-action="skip" is important. If the Learner UI already supplied it, APIM doesn't replace it.
One important improvement: use distributed tracing too
I wouldn't make a custom correlation ID your only tracing mechanism.
Since these are modern .NET microservices, use OpenTelemetry / Activity / W3C Trace Context as the actual distributed tracing mechanism:
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-00f067aa0ba902b7-01

ASP.NET Core and HttpClient already have strong support for this.
Conceptually:
                     TraceId
          4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736
                           │
        ┌──────────────────┼──────────────────┐
        ▼                  ▼                  ▼
    Learner UI         Learner API        Course API
      Span A              Span B             Span C
                            │
                            ▼
                       Progress API
                          Span D

The TraceId stays the same, while each operation gets its own SpanId.
For your LMS architecture, I'd therefore carry both:
X-Correlation-ID: e7253292-41ce-4d8b-93f8-...
traceparent: 00-4bf92f3577b34da6a3ce929d0e0e4736-...

Use X-Correlation-ID as the human/support-friendly request identifier, while OpenTelemetry provides the real distributed trace.
For asynchronous operations through Azure Service Bus, also propagate the correlation/trace context in the message properties:
Learner UI
    ↓
APIM
    ↓
Learner API ──────────────► Progress API
    │
    │ Service Bus message
    ▼
Service Bus
    ↓
Completion Processor
    ↓
Azure SQL / downstream service

      SAME TRACE / CORRELATION CONTEXT

That gives you end-to-end visibility even when the HTTP request ends before the background processing occurs.
For the LMS stack you're building, I would standardize this as a shared NuGet library used by Learner, Trainer, Manager/Admin, and all backend APIs, rather than duplicating correlation middleware and handlers in every service.
